This looks like January 2025. The fed funds rate is 4.33%, which fits the range left after the December 2024 cut. The 10-year yield is 4.55% and the 2-year is 4.24%, and the 10-year rose about 74bp over 3 months, consistent with Sept 2024 to Jan 2025. The unemployment rate is 4.2% (December 2024 was 4.1%, and November was 4.2%). The dollar is up strongly year over year, and gold is up 26.7% over 12 months. Large-cap equity is up 24.9% over 12 months, though the 1m return of -2.4% is odd for January 2025. That return fits December 2024 better, when the S&P fell about 2.4% in the month.

In December 2024 the 10-year ended at 4.57%, the 2-year at 4.24%, and fed funds at 4.33%. The 3-month change in policy rate of -0.50 fits too, since the cuts were Sept 50bp, Nov 25bp, and Dec 25bp. Between end-September and end-December that is a change of -0.50, which matches. Unemployment was 4.2% in November 2024 and 4.1% in December, so the 4.2% is likely the latest value as published at the time. Core CPI at 3.3% and headline at 2.73% match November 2024 (2.7% headline, 3.3% core). So the snapshot is December 2024.

